\begin{abstract}
Contact between fluctuating, fluid-lubricated soft surfaces is prevalent in engineering and biological systems, a process starting with adhesive contact, which can give rise to complex coarsening dynamics. One representation of such a system, which is relevant to biological membrane adhesion, is a fluctuating elastic interface covered by adhesive molecules that bind and unbind to a solid substrate across a narrow gap filled with a viscous fluid. This flow is described by the stochastic elastohydrodynamics thin-film equation, which combines the effects of viscous nanometric thin film flow, elastic membrane properties, adhesive springs, and thermal fluctuations. The average time it takes the fluctuating elastic membrane to adhere is predicted by the rare event theory, increasing exponentially with the square of the initial gap height. Numerical simulations reveal a phase separation of membrane domains driven by the binding and unbinding of adhesive molecules. The coarsening process displays close similarities to classical Ostwald ripening; however, the inclusion of hydrodynamics affects power-law growth. In particular, we identify a new bending-dominated coarsening regime, which is slower than the well-known tension-dominated case.
%Many important physiological processes require cells forming direct contacts, as well as the coarsening of adhesion patches. To investigate these phenomena, we established a stochastic elastohydrodynamics thin-film equation that includes four key physical components: the nanometric thin layer of viscous fluid between cells, the elastic properties of cell membranes, adhesive molecules that may bind across the viscous layer, and stochastic fluctuations. Direct contacts are made when thermal fluctuations bring the cell membranes close enough for the adhesive molecules to bind. Through numerical simulations, we find that the average adhesion time increases exponentially with the square of the separation distance, as predicted by rare-event theory. Our simulations recreate the phase separation of membrane domains driven by binding and unbinding of adhesive molecules observed in various biological systems. We show that this physical process is mathematically similar to other coarsening processes such as Ostwald ripening, and observe that the characteristic length scale exhibits power-law growth. Our exploration of novel coarsening regimes demonstrates that coarsening is slower than the well-known $\sim t^{1/3}$ power law when it is driven by bending instead of tension, and that the viscous resistance to fluid flow leads to slower coarsening when the initial film height is increased.
\end{abstract}
