\begin{abstract}
%This paper investigates when discrete choice data with aggregated alternatives of ambiguous composition—such as grouped products or outside options—can be rationalized by a random utility model (RUM). We provide a theoretical characterization of the observable implications of RUMs under aggregation and show that these implications are surprisingly weak: they consist only of a monotonicity condition with respect to adding aggregated alternatives and standard RUM consistency on menus without aggregation. These conditions are not sufficient to justify estimation using an aggregated RUM—a common practice in the empirical literature. We identify two additional conditions—non-overlapping preferences and menu-independent aggregation—that are sufficient for full rationalizability by an aggregated RUM. Simulation results confirm that violations of these conditions lead to estimation bias, underscoring the practical importance of carefully defining aggregated alternatives.

This paper studies when discrete choice data involving aggregated alternatives such as categorical data or an outside option can be rationalized by a random utility model (RUM). Aggregation introduces ambiguity in composition: the underlying alternatives may differ across individuals and remain unobserved by the analyst. We characterize the observable implications of RUMs under such ambiguity and show that they are surprisingly weak, implying only monotonicity with respect to adding aggregated alternatives and standard RUM consistency on unaggregated menus. These are insufficient to justify the use of an aggregated RUM. We identify two sufficient conditions that restore full rationalizability: non-overlapping preferences and menu-independent aggregation. Simulations show that violations of these conditions generate estimation bias, highlighting the practical importance of how aggregated alternatives are defined.
\end{abstract}




\section{Introduction}


In empirical studies of discrete choice, it is common practice to aggregate alternatives. For instance, in car sales data, all variants of the Toyota RAV4 despite differing in features, prices, and availability, are often grouped under a single label, “RAV4.” A particularly important form of aggregation involves {\it the outside option}. In many empirical industrial organization (IO) studies such as \cite{nevo2001measuring}, \cite{petrin2004cable}, \cite{shum2016econometric}, among many others\footnote{We provide a detailed explanation of the setup in \cite{nevo2001measuring} later in the paper. In \cite{petrin2004cable}, the outside option refers to households that do not subscribe to cable. \cite{shum2016econometric} is a textbook that incorporates the outside option as a central feature of the standard discrete choice model.}, the outside option is modeled as a single alternative that encompasses all unlisted or non-participating choices. For example, in \cite{nevo2001measuring}, which studies consumer demand for 25 cereal brands, the market share of the outside good is defined as  residual: the difference between one and the total market share of the inside goods, implicitly capturing all other breakfast options, such as pancakes or omelets.\footnote{See Appendix A: Data, page 337.}


The standard modeling approach assumes a random utility model (RUM) defined directly over the aggregated alternatives—what we call the {\it aggregated RUM}. That is, researchers assume that each aggregated alternative corresponds to a single alternative, and that preferences are defined over these aggregated labels as if they were atomic options. An aggregated RUM is then modeled as a probability distribution over such preference orderings. This the standard framework in the discrete choice literature.



%The problem is that, from the consumer’s perspective, each version may differ substantially in preference ranking. Moreover, some versions (e.g., the most expensive one) may not be available to certain consumers due to constraints like income or geographic availability, which may not be observable to the analyst.

%As in the car example, a key issue is that both the desirability and availability of the outside option can vary across consumers, depending on unobserved individual characteristics like income, location, or lifestyle, which may not be observable to the analyst.


However, this modeling simplification obscures an important complication: while the analyst only observes aggregate frequencies, the consumer may evaluate the alternatives composing an aggregated alternative very differently. In the car example, different versions of the RAV4 such as the Gasoline LE and the Plug-in Hybrid XSE may vary significantly in both desirability and affordability. Likewise, the outside option may encompass alternatives that differ widely in price, quality, and accessibility. Furthermore, the exact composition of an aggregated alternative (the set of alternatives available to the consumer) may be unobservable to the analyst. As a result, observed choices frequencies over aggregated alternatives may not represent consumers' underlying preferences.


This disconnect between observed data and actual choice behavior introduces what we call the {\it ambiguity of composition}—the fact that each aggregated alternative is a composite whose internal structure is unknown to the analyst but potentially crucial for understanding decision-making.

%Our paper is the first to formally study the implications of ambiguity in the composition of aggregated alternatives. We aim to characterize the observable implications of the RUM in the presence of aggregation. We show that these implications are too weak to justify even the use of an aggregated RUM. Through a simulation exercise, we further demonstrate that such weak implications can result in estimation biases. Building on these findings, we provide theoretical results that provide guidance on how to define aggregated alternatives in ways that mitigate these biases.


Our paper is the first to formally study the implications of this ambiguity in the composition of aggregated alternatives. We characterize the observable implications of the random utility model (RUM) under the ambiguity of composition caused by  aggregation, and show that these implications are generally too weak to justify even the use of an aggregated RUM. Through a simulation exercise, we further demonstrate that this weakness can lead to significant estimation biases. Building on these findings, we provide theoretical results that offer guidance on how to define aggregated alternatives in ways that mitigate such biases.




%Accordingly, the relevant random utility model should also be defined over the underlying alternatives.

%Our paper is the first to theoretically investigate the problem by focusing on the ambiguity inherent in the composition of aggregated alternatives, although several empirical papers have raised concerns about potential biases introduced by aggregation without referring to the ambiguity. 



\subsection{Preview of theoretical results}

To illustrate our contribution, consider a similar setup that studied in \cite{nevo2001measuring}, which involves 25 cereal brands (e.g., Kellogg Corn Flakes, Kellogg Crispix, $\ldots$, GM Kix, GM Trix, Quaker Life) along with an outside good. For simplicity, suppose the outside good comprises two alternatives---\textit{pancakes} and \textit{omelette}---denoted collectively by ``out''. Importantly, the composition of the outside good may differ across individuals in the population. For instance, some consumers may not consider omelette a viable option due to egg shortages resulting from bird flu, while others may rule out pancakes because essential ingredients such as syrup are unavailable in stores. %The ambiguity in the composition arises because this variation may not be observable to the analyst.


To formally capture this variation  in the composition of aggregated alternatives, we introduce the concept of an \emph{aggregation rule}, denoted by~$\lambda$. The aggregation rule specifies, for each choice set, a probability distribution over the possible compositions of aggregated alternatives. In the cereal example, this means that for any menu including the outside option, $\lambda$ determines the probability that a consumer faces only pancakes, only omelette, or both as the components of the outside option. More generally, $\lambda$ reflects the distribution of available underlying alternatives across individuals in the population, and may vary with the choice set to capture differences in availability across markets or contexts. Crucially, $\lambda$ is not observed by the analyst, and this unobservability is what gives rise to the ambiguity of composition.




We say that the observed dataset~$\hat{\rho}$ is consistent with a RUM if there exists (i) an aggregation rule~$\lambda$ and (ii) a probability distribution~$\mu$ over rankings (i.e., linear orders) ~$\succ$ on the underlying alternatives (in this case, the 25 cereal brands along with omelette and pancakes), such that for all subsets $D$ of cereals and $x \in D$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:examp}
\begin{aligned}
\hat{\rho}(D \cup \text{out}, x) &= \lambda_{D \cup {\text{out}}}(\{\text{omelette}\}) \, \mu(\succ \mid x \succ y \text{ for all } y \in D \cup \{\text{omelette}\}) \\
&\quad + \lambda_{D \cup {\text{out}}}(\{\text{pancakes}\}) \, \mu(\succ \mid x \succ y \text{ for all } y \in D \cup \{\text{pancakes}\}) \\
&\quad + \lambda_{D \cup {\text{out}}}(\{\text{omelette}, \text{pancakes}\}) \, \mu(\succ \mid x \succ y \text{ for all } y \in D \cup \{\text{omelette}, \text{pancakes}\}),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_{D \cup {\text{out}}}(E)$ is the probability that the outside option out is composed of the set $E \subseteq \{\text{omelette}, \text{pancakes}\}$ in the context of choice set $D \cup {\text{out}}$. As mentioned, in principle,~$\lambda$ may vary with the choice set. For instance, in practice, the set of available alternatives often depends on which store the consumer chooses to visit. 


%For instance, in \cite{nevo2001measuring}, each choice set corresponds to a specific city and quarter, and the availability or attractiveness of components of the outside good---such as the availavility of  eggs---can vary across time and location.



Our first question is: \textit{What are the observable implications for~$\hat{\rho}$ that are consistent with some pair~$(\mu, \lambda)$ as described above?} We show that these implications are characterized by two conditions. The first is a \textit{monotonicity condition with respect to adding aggregated alternatives}---that is, the choice frequency of a non-aggregated alternative decreases when an aggregated alternative is added to the choice set. The second is a relatively trivial condition that we call {\it partial RU rationality}, that is on the subset of choice sets that do not contain the aggregated alternative, choices must be rationalizable by a standard RUM. See Theorems \ref{thm:1} and \ref{thm:1multiple} in Section \ref{sec:characterization} for the formal statements. 



Crucially, these two conditions are \textit{insufficient} to guarantee the existence of an \textit{aggregated RUM}. That is, a distribution over preference rankings on \textit{aggregated} alternatives, which is typically assumed in empirical applications. The reason is that such a model would additionally require monotonicity with respect to adding \textit{non-aggregated} alternatives---that is, the choice frequency of aggregated alternatives should also decrease when a new non-aggregated alternative is added to the choice set. This gap is especially concerning given how commonly aggregated RUMs are used in empirical work. 

Given this first result, we turn to our second question: \textit{Under what additional conditions on the underlying RUM can we recover the full set of implications associated with an aggregated RUM from the observed choice data~$\hat{\rho}$?} This second question is also practically important, as it provides guidance on how to define aggregated alternatives when constructing datasets for empirical analysis using the aggregated RUM.


 We identify two simple conditions under which the full implications of the aggregated RUM can be recovered. The first condition on $\mu$ holds when all consumers in the population  perceive all variants of an aggregated alternative as similar in terms of their rankings. More precisely, the condition requires that no non-aggregated alternative falls between any two components of the aggregated alternative in a consumer's preference ranking. We call it the {\it non-overlapping} condition. In the example of cereal choice, this condition implies that no consumer ranks any of the cereals between omelette and pancakes.  This assumption may be violated if, for instance, some consumers prefer omelets to certain cereals but still prefer other cereals to pancakes. This introduces overlap between the components of the outside option and the inside goods.  See Proposition \ref{thm:non-overlapping} in Section \ref{sec:restrictions} for the formal statements. 


The second condition requires that the aggregation rule~$\lambda$ be independent of choice sets (i.e., menus). In typical empirical IO settings, each choice set corresponds to a different market. Hence, this condition implies that~$\lambda$ must remain constant across markets. Returning to the setup of cereal choice, this would mean, for example, that if in one market eggs are unavailable but the ingredients for pancakes are available to $\alpha$ percent of the population, eggs are available but pancake ingredients are not for $\beta$ percent, and both are available for the remaining fraction, then this same distribution must hold in all other markets. This assumption may be violated in practice. For instance, if egg availability or prices vary by market, $\lambda$ may be menu-dependent. 
%Thus, the second condition cautions against aggregating alternatives whose choice frequencies or interpretations vary substantially across markets. 
See Proposition \ref{thm:independent} in Section \ref{sec:restrictions} for the formal statements. 

