\subsection{Simulation results}

Our findings suggest that as the underlying model deviates from these two conditions (non-overlapping condition and menut independence), the resulting choice frequencies will increasingly diverge from those predicted by the aggregated RUM. Consequently, if one estimates a model assuming the validity of an aggregated RUM (e.g., a logit model), the parameter estimates may exhibit significant bias. Empirically validating this conjecture is critical for assessing the practical importance of our theoretical results.


To assess the empirical relevance of our theoretical results, we conducted the following simulation-based analysis. We varied either the aggregation rule~$\lambda$ or the preference structure, holding all other parameters fixed, and examined the resulting estimation bias. We assumed that the underlying true model is a logit  model. Based on this assumption, we first generated the original choice data~$\rho$ assuming the true utility values. Then, given fixed values of~$\lambda$ (along with all other parameters), we constructed a reduced dataset~$\hat{\rho}$ from~$\rho$. Using~$\hat{\rho}$, we estimated utility values under the assumption that $\hat{\rho}$ follows a logit model. It is important to remember that when~$\lambda$ is menu-independent or the preference structure is non-overlapping, the reduced dataset~$\hat{\rho}$ is consistent with an aggregated RUM. Otherwise, there is no guarantee that~$\hat{\rho}$ is a RUM, let alone the logit form; thus the estimation is misspecified. 

As our estimation target, we focused on the utility ratio between two non-aggregated alternatives, specifically~$\exp(u(x))/\exp(u(y))$. The estimation bias was measured as the deviation between this true ratio and the estimated ratio:
\begin{equation}
\exp \left| (\hat{u}(x) - \hat{u}(y)) - (u(x) - u(y)) \right|.
\end{equation}
In addition, we computed the geometric \textit{distance} from~$\hat{\rho}$ to the set of RUMs.

Our findings show that both the estimation bias and the geometric distance increase as~$\lambda$ becomes more market-dependent or the preference structure becomes more overlapping. These results align with our theoretical predictions and underscore the empirical importance of the conditions required for the validity of aggregated RUMs. Further details of our simulation are provided in Section \ref{sec:simulation}.




\subsection{Related Literature}



%The mapping from true outcomes to observed subsets may be deterministic or stochastic. While related, our setup differs from this literature in important ways. That literature considers uncertainty—if any—only in the coarsening process, as in \cite{heitjan1990}. In contrast, in our framework, each aggregated alternative randomly consists of a subset of underlying alternatives. 

There is no paper studying stochastic choice focusing on ambiguity in the composition of aggregated alternatives. There are, however,  several papers that are related in terms of mathematical setup or motivation. 


The first line of research related to our paper is the recent work on random attention models, such as \cite{masatlioglu2020ram}, \cite{aguiar2022attention}, \cite{rehbeck2016attention},%\cite{barseghyan2021ecma},
\cite{manzinimariotti2014ecma},and  \cite{horan2019default}. See \cite{tomasztextbook} for a survey of the literature.  These papers study models in which agents do not consider the full available choice set but instead randomly consider a subset. In particular, \cite{horan2019default} studies the case where the choice frequency of the outside or ``default'' option is not observable under the random attention model.

In our model, randomness in the composition of aggregated alternatives can be interpreted as randomness in the alternatives an agent considers. While the mathematical structures are related, the motivation and mechanisms differ significantly. We focus on the stochastic availability of alternatives due to aggregation, whereas the cited papers are motivated by attention or cognitive constraints. As a result, the assumptions on the distribution over available alternatives also differ. In particular, we assume that at least one alternative from each aggregated set is available to the agent. For example, if a choice set contains ``RAV4", then at least one of the version of RAV4 should be available to the consumer. 

 %\cite{horan2019default} is also related to our discussion of the outside option.  This differs from our model since in our model the aggregate choice probabilities are always observable. Furthermore, Horan focuses on the independent random consideration model proposed by \cite{manzinimariotti2014ecma} which is different from our model as previously mentioned.





\cite{rehbeck2022marginal} also considers a mathematically related model, though with a very different motivation. That paper aims to characterize marginal stochastic choice (i.e., choice frequencies not conditioned on particular choice sets) under the assumption that the analyst observes the distribution over choice sets faced by the agent, but not the agent’s stochastic choice within each set. This contrasts with our framework, in which the distribution over available alternatives is unobserved and represented by an unknown distribution~$\lambda$. Moreover, in our setting, we observe reduced stochastic choice from observed choice sets of aggregated alternatives, even though the  stochastic choice over underlying alternatives is not directly observable. While \cite{rehbeck2022marginal} also considers the case of endogenous menu availability, their focus is on specific models such as those of \cite{gul2001temptation} and \cite{kreps1979flexibility}. Their analysis is concerned with rationalizability, and does not address issues of estimation bias.


\cite{apesteguia2016stochastic} also considers aggregation of stochastic choice data but for a different motivation. They analyze stochastic choice data resulting from the aggregation of individual choice behavior and consider whether the aggregate choice behavior remains in the same class as its constituents'. Their model and motivation differ significantly from ours. While they consider aggregation on the individual level, our model aggregates alternatives and includes aggregation from agents facing different choice problems. 



Our companion paper \cite{kono2025unobservable} also examines the implications of the RUM in the presence of an outside option in a different setup. The paper do not analyze the case of multiple aggregated alternatives. From the perspective of the observed dataset, the key distinction between the two papers is that the present study takes the reduced dataset~$\hat{\rho}$ as the observed object, whereas the companion paper treats the full dataset~$\rho$ (with missing information) as given. Moreover, in the companion paper, the reduced dataset~$\hat{\rho}$ is constructed under the assumption that the outside option always corresponds to the full set of underlying alternatives. In other words, that paper assumes that~$\lambda$ is independent of the choice set and takes the form of a degenerate distribution over the entire set of alternatives that the outside option may represent.\footnote{For instance, in the example introduced earlier, \cite{kono2025unobservable} assumes that the outside option always means both omelette and pancakes.} One of the main findings in \cite{kono2025unobservable} is that even in this idealized case—where~$\lambda$ is degenerate and independent—some implications of the RUM may still be lost. In the present paper, we extend that framework by allowing~$\lambda$ to be non-degenerate or non-independent. In the present paper, we characterize the observable implications of the RUM on the reduced dataset under this more general specification.\footnote{Importantly, we show that the case in which~$\lambda$ is independent and constant represents the best-case scenario—one in which the full implications of the aggregated RUM are preserved and estimation bias is minimized. The more restrictive setup in \cite{kono2025unobservable} thus serves as a natural benchmark for analyzing the more general framework developed here.}


A few papers in the empirical IO literature such as \cite{brownstone2017broad}, \cite{stafford2018fishing}, and \cite{huangoutside}have examined potential biases arising from aggregation and misspecification of the outside option. More recently, \cite{Zhang2023identification} analyzed biases resulting from misspecifying market size, and hence the market share of the outside good. However, all of these papers are purely empirical in nature and do not address the issue of ambiguity of the composition of aggregated alternatives or outside options.

Although less directly related, classical statistical work has developed a comprehensive framework for making inferences from data that are partially observed or categorized. For example, see \cite{heitjan1989} for a review of grouped data; \cite{blumenthal1968} and \cite{nordheim1984} for categorized data; and \cite{heitjan1990, heitjan1991} for coarse data. In this framework, subsets of the sample space are observed rather than exact realizations.\footnote{For example, if the sample space is the real line (e.g., blood pressure), we may only observe a random variable taking interval values (e.g., an interval of blood pressures containing the true value).} A key distinction from our setting is that, in our model, uncertainty arises from the actual choice set an agent faces, rather than from the data collection or coarsening process.





\section{Setup}

\subsection{Alternatives}

We introduce two notations that we use throughout the paper: For any set $Z$, let $\L(Z)$ be the set of rankings (i.e., linear orders) on $Z$ and let $\Delta(Z)$ be the set of probability distributions over $Z$. 

Let ${\A}=\{a_0, .... , a_{N}\}$ be the set of alternatives. Let $\X$ be the set of underlying alternatives.  For each $a \in {\mathscr A}$, let $X(a) \subseteq \X$ be a non-empty finite set of  underlying alternatives that $a$ can represent. We assume that $X(a_i) \cap X(a_j)= \emptyset$ for any distinct $i, j$. 



We say that $a \in {\mathscr A}$ is {\it non-aggregated } if $X(a)$ is singleton; $a \in {\mathscr A}$ is {\it aggregated } if $X(a)$ is not a singleton.  Let $\A_A \subseteq {\mathscr A}$ be the set of aggregated alternatives; $\A_N \subseteq {\mathscr A}$ be the set of non-aggregated alternatives. Let 
\[
\tilde{X} \equiv {\bigcup_{a \in \A_N}} X(a)
\]
be the set of underlying alternatives that  all non-aggregated alternatives represent. Let 
\[
X^* \equiv \bigcup_{a \in \A_A} X(a)
\]
be the set of underlying alternatives that all aggregated alternatives represent.


