\textbf{Change the example using the outside option}



As mentioned, the available dataset is often aggregated, while the underlying preference relation should be defined on the set of underlying alternatives (i.e., $\tilde{X} \cup X^*$). To rationalize the aggregated stochastic choice $\hat{\rho}$ by a random utility model on $X$, we need to consider what the aggregated alternative means in each choice set.  For example, in the choice set $\{\text{Toyota RAV 4}, \text{Tesla Model Y}\}$,  Toyota RAV 4 may mean Hybrid SE by $10\%$ and Plug-in hybrid XSE by $90\%$ in the observable dataset. 
\end{comment}


In our framework, consumers’ preferences are defined over the full set of underlying alternatives, $\tilde{X} \cup X^*$. This distinction becomes important when the components of an aggregated alternative differ meaningfully. For example, if omelets and pancakes vary in availability or appeal across individuals, then modeling them as a single outside option may obscure how consumers actually evaluate their choices. Thus, although the observed dataset $\hat{\rho}$ is defined over aggregated alternatives, the actual choices made by agents reflect preferences over the underlying alternatives in $\tilde{X} \cup X^*$. Accordingly, the relevant random utility model should also be defined over $\tilde{X} \cup X^*$.

To connect these two levels of analysis, we introduce a formal mapping—called an \emph{aggregation rule}—which links the aggregated alternatives in the data to the underlying alternatives in their composition. This mapping $\lambda$ captures the distribution of available underlying alternatives faced by agents in the population, which may not be observable to the analyst. Hence, the ambiguity of composition is driven by unobservability of $\lambda$.


\begin{definition}
A set of functions $(\lambda_A)_{A \subseteq \A}$ is called {\it aggregation rule} if for any $A \subseteq {\mathscr A}$
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\lambda_{A}((S_a)_{a \in A})\ge 0$ for any $(S_a)_{a \in A} \in \prod_{a \in \A}(2^{X(a)}\setminus \emptyset)$,
    \item $\sum_{(S_a)_{a \in A}\in \prod_{a \in E} (2^{X(a) }\setminus \emptyset)}\lambda_{A}((S_a)_{a \in A})=1$.
\end{itemize} 
\end{definition}



The value of the aggregation function $\lambda_{A}((S_a)_{a \in A})$ represents the frequency with which each aggregated alternative $a$ is composed of the subset $S_a \subseteq X(a)$ within the choice set $A \subseteq \mathscr{A}$. As discussed in the introduction, we allow the aggregation rule $\lambda$ to depend on the choice set $A$, reflecting the fact that the availability of underlying alternatives may vary across markets—for example, depending on which stores consumers have access to.

For instance, in the case of Example \ref{ex:nevo}, eggs may be unavailable in one market where alternatives $A$ are available (e.g. due to a bird flu outbreak), making omelettes infeasible, whereas pancake ingredients remain widely available. In this case, the aggregation rule $\lambda_{A}$ would assign a higher probability to $S_{\text{out}} = {\{\text{pancakes}\}}$ than to sets including omelettes. In another market, the situation may be reversed or both items may be available, leading to a different distribution over $S_{\text{out}}$. This variation justifies allowing $\lambda$ to be menu-dependent.


We now define the notion of RU-rationality in the presence of ambiguity of composition.

\begin{definition}\label{def:rationalization}
A stochastic choice function $\hat{\rho}$ is RU-rational if there exists $\mu \in \Delta(\L (\tilde{X} \cup X^*))$ and an aggregation rule $\lambda$ such that for any $a \in A \subseteq {\mathscr A}$, 
\begin{align}\label{eq:rationalization}
\hat{\rho}(A,a) = \sum_{(S_a)_{a \in A}\in \prod_{a\in A} (2^{X(a)}\setminus \emptyset)} \lambda_{A}((S_a)_{a \in A})\mu(\succ \in \L (\tilde{X} \cup X^*) | \exists x \in S_a, \ \forall y \in \cup_{b \in A \setminus a} S_b, \ x \succ y).
\end{align}
\end{definition}
We say that the pair $(\mu, \lambda)$ {\it RU-rationalizes} $\hat\rho$. See equation~\eqref{eq:examp} in the introduction for a concrete instance of equation~\eqref{eq:rationalization} in the cereal choice example. Note that in the right hand side of (\ref{eq:rationalization}), the condition on $\succ$ states that there exists an underlying alternative $x$ that $a$ represents that dominates all underlying alternatives that all elements in  $A \setminus a$ represent.  


\begin{remark}
Definition~\ref{def:rationalization} assumes independence between the distribution~$\mu$ over preference rankings and the aggregation rule~$\lambda$ over underlying alternatives. While one could consider a more general framework allowing dependence between these components, such a model may lack testable implications, since the joint distribution could vary with the menu as $\lambda$ does. In particular, such dependence would allow the distribution over rankings to vary with the menu, rendering the model unfalsifiable.

%we focus on this simplified structure. This is both for tractability—given that this paper is the first to formally study the problem—and because, as our characterization results demonstrate, even this simple model yields surprisingly weak observable implications.
\end{remark}


It is easy to observe that aggregated RU rationality implies RU rationality.  

\begin{remark} To see why, suppose $\hat\rho$ is rationalized by $\hat\mu$ over aggregated alternatives $\A$. For each $a \in \A$, arbitrarily choose an $x_a \in X(a)$ to represent $a$. Then let $\lambda$ be such that $a$ always means $\{x_a\}$ and define $\mu$ from $\hat\mu$ in the natural way. That is, for each $\hat\succ$ in the support of $\hat\mu$ define $\succ$ by $x_a \succ x_b$ whenever $a \ \hat\succ \ b$ and all other comparisons arbitrary. Then by setting $\mu(\succ) = \hat\mu (\hat\succ)$ for all $\hat\succ$ in the support of $\hat\mu$ it is apparent that $(\mu,\lambda)$ rationalizes $\hat\rho$.
\end{remark}


We define one more concept of rationality.

\begin{definition}
A stochastic choice function $\hat{\rho}$ is {\it partially RU-rational}\footnote{Equivalently, we can define partial RU rationality by the existence of  $\mu \in \Delta(\A_N)$ such that for any $a \in A  \subseteq \A_N$, $\hat{\rho}(A,a)= \mu(\succ \in \L(\A)| a \succ b \text{ for all }b \in A \setminus a)$. We adopt the definition by (\ref{eq:partial_ru}), as it is easier to see RU rationality implies partial RU rationality.} if there exists $\mu  \in \Delta (\tilde{X})$ such that for any $a \in A \subseteq \A_N$
\begin{align}\label{eq:partial_ru}
\hat{\rho}(A,a) = \mu(\succ \in \L (\tilde{X}) | \forall y \in \cup_{b \in A \setminus a}X(b), x \succ y  \text{ where }X(a)=\{x\}).
\end{align}
\end{definition}

Note that the definition above applies to choice sets composed entirely of non-aggregated alternatives (i.e., $A \subseteq \A_N$). In this case, equation \eqref{eq:partial_ru} coincides with equation \eqref{eq:rationalization} because each $X(a)$ is a singleton and thus $S_a = X(a)$. In this sense, partial RU-rationality is simply RU-rationality restricted to non-aggregated alternatives.



\begin{remark}Summarizing the relationship between the three rationality concepts, we have
\[
\text{ Aggregated RU }(\ref{eq:agg_rationalization})\underset{(*)}{\implies} \text{ RU } (\ref{eq:rationalization}) \implies
\text{Partial RU } (\ref{eq:partial_ru}).
\]
As mentioned earlier, aggregated RU-rationality is the standard assumption in much of the empirical literature. The RU-rationality notion introduced in this paper is a natural refinement that accounts for ambiguity in the composition of aggregated alternatives.  
