\section{Characterization}\label{sec:characterization}


In this section, we begin by analyzing the case in which there is only one aggregated alternative. Specifically, we assume that $X(a_0)$ is not a singleton, while $X(a_i)$ is a singleton for all $i \neq 0$. This case is useful for two reasons. First, it reflects the common modeling practice of including an outside option—an aggregated alternative that captures unobserved or unlisted choices—frequently used in empirical applications. Although empirical models often involve more than one aggregated alternative, the outside option is particularly prominent. Second, this simplified setting serves as a foundation for analyzing the more general case with multiple aggregated alternatives.



\begin{theorem}\label{thm:1} Assume that there is only one aggregated alternative $a_0$ (i.e., $\A_A=\{a_0\}$).  Let $\hat{\rho}$ be a stochastic choice function.\\
(i) If $\hat{\rho}$ is RU rational, then
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i-a)] (Monotonicity with respect adding to aggregated alternative (i.e., outside option)): if $b \in D \subseteq \A_N$ then $\hat\rho(D, b) \ge \hat\rho(D \cup \{a_0\},b)$
\item[(i-b)] (Partial RU rationality) $\hat\rho$ is partially RU rational.
\end{itemize}
(ii) Assume that $|X(a_0)| \ge |\tilde{X}| + 1$.\footnote{This means that the number of underlying alternatives the aggregated alternatives $a_0$ can represent is equal to or larger than the number of non-aggreaged alternatives plus one.} Then $\hat{\rho}$ is RU rational if and only if $\hat{\rho}$ satisfies the two conditions above.
\end{theorem}





%Notes: without any lower bound on $|X^*|$ the two conditions are no longer sufficient, because consider $|X^*|=1$. If $|X \setminus X^*| = n$ then the minimal number of inequalities needed to characterize RUM is $(n+1)(2^n-2)$ while condition 1 has $n2^{n-1}$ and condition 2 has $n(2^{n-1}-2)$, or $n(2^n-2)$ in total which is not enough.\\

The necessity of (i-a) and (i-b) is immediate.  The sufficiency part of the proof is constructive and nontrivial.  We provide the intuition for sufficiency in subsection \ref{sec:intuition}. The formal proof is in the appendix.


A key takeaway from the theorem is that the empirical implications of RU-rationality are substantially weaker than those of aggregated RU-rationality. In particular, even the basic monotonicity condition with respect to the addition of \emph{non-aggregated} alternatives may be violated under RU-rationality. To see this, consider a choice set $D$ such that $a_0 \in D$ and $b \notin D$. Under RU-rationality (Definition \ref{def:rationalization}), it is possible to observe
\[
\hat{\rho}(D,a_0)<\hat{\rho}(D\cup\{b\},a_0),
\]
which violates standard monotonicity. This arises because, in the expanded set $D \cup {b}$, the outside option $a_0$ may become more desirable than it was in $D$. For example, in the context of cereal choice, the addition of a low-quality, inexpensive cereal brand may serve as a signal that the market corresponds to a lower-income area—leading to a change in the interpretation or attractiveness of the outside good.

Aggregated RU-rationality, by contrast, imposes much stronger restrictions—including full monotonicity and beyond. When $\hat{\rho}$ is observed on all subsets of $\mathscr{A}$, aggregated RU-rationality is equivalent to the non-negativity of all Block-Marschak (BM) polynomials. Among these, monotonicity is one of the simplest and most intuitive implications. However, as Theorem~\ref{thm:1} shows, RU-rationality satisfies even this basic implication only partially.

This result underscores the substantial gap between RU-rationality and aggregated RU-rationality. In general, RU-rationality imposes significantly weaker structure on observed choice behavior, highlighting the empirical limitations of using aggregated data without further assumptions on how aggregation is formed. In Section~\ref{sec:restrictions}, we explore the specific conditions on the underlying distributions~$\mu$ and~$\lambda$ under which the full implications of an aggregated RUM can be recovered from the observed data~$\hat{\rho}$.






%Second take away is that, the theorems provide guidance on how outside options should be aggregated. Specifically, they indicate that the outside option must be chosen to satisfy the two characterization conditions (i-a) and (i-b) to maintain RU rationality.


Three more technical remarks are in order:



\begin{remark}
More broadly, the reason we do not derive additional implications beyond monotonicity is that, in general, due to the presence of the menu dependent parameter $\lambda$, we lack control over how the addition of a new alternative affects the composition of the outside option. However, when we consider the addition of aggregated alternatives, the situation is different. Regardless of what these aggregated alternatives represent, their addition does not affect the relative desirability of non-aggregated alternatives. Hence, the monotonicity property is preserved in this case.
\end{remark}



