 \subsection{Restriction on $\mu$:  non-overlapping $\mu$}\label{sec:mu}

 In this section we provide a restriction on the distribution of preferences that recovers the implication of aggregated RUM. The restriction says for each aggregated alternative $a$, the underlying alternatives in $X(a)$ are similar in utility.

\begin{definition}
A distribution $\mu \in \Delta (\L(\tilde{X} \cup X^*))$ is non-overlapping  if for any $\succ \in supp (\mu)$, $a \in \A_A$, and $y,z \in X(a)$,
\[
y \succ x \succ z \implies x \in X(a).
\]
\end{definition}
The definition says that any preference in the support of $\mu$ has the property that each $X(a)$ does not overlap with other aggregated alternatives.\footnote{It  is possible to weaken the definition  as follows but we keep this condition for simplicity: A distribution $\mu \in \Delta (\L(\tilde{X} \cup X^*))$ is almost non-overlapping for any $\succ \in supp (\mu)$, there exists $b,c \in \A$ such that (i) $y \succ x$ for all $x \in X(b) \cup X(c)$ and $y \in (\tilde{X} \cup X^*) \setminus (X(b) \cup X(c))$ and (ii) for all  $a \in \A_A \setminus \{b,c\}$, and $y,z \in X(a)$, $y \succ x \succ z \implies x \in X(a)$.}  

%We consider as a special case, non-overlapping $\mu$ which will be useful in the analysis of almost non-overlapping $\mu$.


The following lemma will be analytically useful. It says that if $\mu$ is non-overlapping, every $\lambda$ produces the same aggregated choice function.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:non-overlapping} Let $\hat \rho$ be a stochastsic choice function.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Suppose that  $(\mu, \lambda)$ RU rationalizes $\hat{\rho}$ and $\mu$ is non-overlapping. Then for any aggregation rule $\lambda'$, $(\mu, \lambda')$ rationalizes $\hat{\rho}$.
\item[(b)] Suppose that there is no non-overlapping $\mu$ such that $(\mu, \lambda)$ rationalizes $\hat{\rho}$ for some $\lambda$.  Then, for any aggregation rule $\lambda'$, there does not exist non-overlapping $\mu'$ such that $(\mu', \lambda')$ rationalizes $\hat{\rho}$.
\end{itemize}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} Statement (a) follows from the fact that if $\mu$ is non-overlapping then for all $(S_a)_{a \in A}\in \prod_{a \in A} (2^{X(a) }\setminus \emptyset)$ and $(S'_a)_{a \in 
A}\in \prod_{a \in A}  (2^{X(a) }\setminus \emptyset)$ we have that $\rho^\mu(\cup_{a \in A} S_a, S_b)  = \rho^\mu(\cup_{a \in A} S'_a,S'_b )$ for all for all $b \in A$ where $\rho^\mu$ is the underlying choice function rationalized by $\mu$.  
Fix $(S^*_a)_{a \in A}$. Then for all $b \in A$, we have 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{(S_a)_{a \in A}\in \prod_{a \in A} (2^{X(a) }\setminus \emptyset)} \lambda_{A}((S_a)_{a \in A})\rho^\mu(\cup_{a \in A} S_a,S_b) &= \rho^\mu(\cup_{a \in A} S^*_a,S^*_b)\sum_{(S_a)_{a \in A}\in \prod_{a \in A} (2^{X(a) }\setminus \emptyset)} \lambda_{A}((S_a)_{a \in A})\\
&= \rho^\mu(\cup_{a \in A} S^*_a,S^*_b)\\
&=\mu(\succ | \exists x \in S^*_b, \ \forall y \in \cup_{a \in A \setminus b} S_a, \ x \succ y).
\end{align*}
Thus every $\lambda$ produces the same choice function. Thus, by the definition of rationalization (Definition \ref{def:rationalization}), we established statement (a). 



To prove statement (b), assume there is no non-overlapping $\mu$ that RU rationalizes $\hat{\rho}$. By way of contradiction assume that there exist $\lambda'$  and non-overlapping $\mu'$ such that $(\mu', \lambda')$ that rationalizes $\hat{\rho}$.  Given this, statement (a) implies a contradiction.
\end{proof}
Lemma \ref{lem:non-overlapping} says that if the underlying $\mu$ is non-overlapping, the observed aggregated choice frequencies are independent of $\lambda$. Based on this intuition, the next result says that non-overlapping $\mu$ justify the use of an aggregated RUM.

