\begin{proposition}
\label{thm:non-overlapping}
A stochastic choice function $\hat{\rho}$ is RU raitonal by a non-overlapping $\mu$ if and only if $\hat{\rho}$ is aggregated RU rational.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Only if direction: It suffices to show this for the case where $\mu$ is deterministic. Suppose $\mu$ is  non-overlapping and $\mu(\succ) = 1$ for some $\succ \in \L(\tilde{X} \cup X^*)$ and fix $\lambda$.  Label the elements of $\A $ as $a_1,a_2,...a_{|\A|}$ in such a way that if $i<j$ then $x \succ y$ for all $x \in X(a_i)$ and $y \in X(a_j)$. This labeling is possible by the almost non-overlapping condition.\\
Let $\hat\succ$  be defined such that 
\[a_1 \ \hat{\succ} \ a_2 \ \hat{\succ} \ \cdots a_{|\A|}. 
\]
It is easy to see that if $D \subseteq \A$ contains any element of $\A$ then $\hat{\rho}$ puts probability $1$ on the $a_i$ with the largest index. Thus, $\hat{\mu}$ rationalizes $\hat{\rho}$.


If direction: There exists $\hat{\mu} \in \Delta ({\mathscr L}( {\mathscr A}))$ that rationalizes $\hat{\rho}$. 
For each $\hat{\succ} \in \text{supp }\hat{\mu}$ consider a ranking $\succ \in {\mathcal L}(\tilde{X} \cup X^*)$ that extends  $\hat{\succ}$ by 
\[
x \succ y \text{ if } x \in X(a) \text{ and } y \in X(b) \text{ for some } a \  \hat{\succ}\  b
\]
and fill the order among $X(a)$ arbitrarily for all $a$; then define $\mu(\succ)=\hat{\mu}(\hat{\succ})$. Also define $\lambda$ arbitrarily. Since $\mu$ is non-overlapping, it follows from statement (b)  of Lemma \ref{lem:non-overlapping} that  $(\mu,\lambda)$ rationalizes $\hat{\rho}$.  
\end{proof}


Proposition \ref{thm:non-overlapping} establishes that, without any assumptions on $\lambda$, the aggregate RU model is valid only when the alternatives grouped within each aggregate occupy similar positions in agents’ preference rankings. Consequently, when constructing an aggregated data set, alternatives with closely related characteristics should not be assigned to different aggregates. A similar observation has been made empirically by \citet{stafford2018fishing} using a dataset of lobster fishing. In particular, she demonstrated  that classifying crab-fishing sites as part of the outside option leads to biased coefficient estimates. Because lobster and crab fishing are close substitutes, an excellent lobster site is preferred to a poor crab site and vice-versa, their rankings overlap, thereby violating the non-overlapping condition specified in Proposition \ref{thm:non-overlapping}.



