\subsection{Restriction on $\lambda$: menu-independent $\lambda$}\label{sec:la}

The second condition that we discuss is that the distribution of aggregated alternatives is the same across choice sets, or markets. 


\begin{definition}
An aggregation rule $\lambda$ is said to be {\it  menu independent} if there exists  $\lambda \in \Delta\Big( \prod_{a \in \A} (2^{X(a)}\setminus \emptyset)\Big)$ such that for any $B\subset {\mathscr A}$ and $(S_a)_{a \in B} \in \prod_{a \in B} (
2^{X(a)} \setminus \emptyset)$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:def_ind}
\lambda_{B}((S_a)_{a \in B})= \lambda\Big(\prod_{a \in B} (S_a) \times \prod_{a \in \A \setminus B} (2^{X(a)}\setminus \emptyset)\Big).
\end{equation}
\end{definition}


\begin{remark} To explain the meaning of our condition,  consider a stronger condition as follows:  there exists  $\lambda_{a} \in \Delta(2^{X(a)}\setminus \emptyset)$ for each $a \in {\mathscr A}$ such that for any $B\subset {\mathscr A}$ and $(S_a)_{a \in B} \in \prod_{a \in B} (
2^{X(a)} \setminus \emptyset)$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:strong_ind}
\lambda_{B}((S_a)_{a \in B})= \prod_{a \in B} \lambda_{a}(S_a).
\end{equation}
The condition (\ref{eq:def_ind}) says that there exists a joint distribution that works across choice sets.\footnote{For example, consider $a, b \in \A_A$ and let $X(a)=\{x_1, y_1\}$ and $X(b)=\{x_2, y_2\}$. Definition \ref{def:rationalization}  allows the situation that $a$ means alternative $x_1$ ($y_1$) if and only if $b$ means alternative $x_2$  ($y_2$, respectively); and  $a$ means $x_1$ and $y_1$ equally likely.  In this example, the probability that $a$ means $x_1$ and $b$ means $y_2$ is zero, which is not allowed in (\ref{eq:strong_ind}).}
%\footnote{Our condition is slightly stronger than the following: for all $D, D' \subset \A_N, E \subset \A_A$ for all $S_a \subset X(a)$ for all $a \in E$  $\lambda_{D \cup E}((S_a)_{a \in E})= \lambda_{D' \cup E}((S_a)_{a \in E})$. The difference is that the new one requires the existence of the joint distribution. They can be equivalent. } 
 (\ref{eq:def_ind}) is weaker than condition (\ref{eq:strong_ind}) in the sense that it allows correlation across aggregated alternatives. The two conditions are equivalent when there is only one aggregate alternative.
\end{remark}



In some markets, $\lambda$ may be independent of the menu. For example, in the automobile market, if cars are aggregated by brand, the distribution of models within each brand is likely to remain relatively constant regardless of the presence of other brands. In contrast, for other categories of goods, $\lambda$ may exhibit menu dependence. Consider grocery stores that aggregate products by food type such as cereals, vegetables, meats, and so forth. The composition of these aggregated alternatives can vary significantly across supermarkets. For instance, the presence of some food types may indicate a low-income area, associated with lower quality vegetables, meats, and so forth. %For instance, a greater number of aggregated alternatives may reflect a larger store, where each category encompasses a wider array of underlying options.
 %For example, in the cereal market where the outside option is all available foods apart from cereal, the presence of certain cereals may indicate that the quality of outside goods is lower.


When $\lambda$ is independent,  representation (\ref{eq:rationalization}) in Definition \ref{def:rationalization}  can be simplified as in the following lemma.

\begin{comment}
\[
\hat{\rho}(D\cup \{x^*_i\}_{i \in I}, x)=\begin{cases} \displaystyle \sum_{(E_i)_{i \in I}\in \prod_{i \in I} 2^{X^*_i}\setminus \emptyset}
\Big( \prod_{i \in I} \lambda_{x^*_i}(E_i) \Big) \rho(D \cup \bigcup_i E_i,x) & \text{if } x \in \tilde{X}\\
\displaystyle \sum_{(E_i)_{i \in I}\in \prod_{i \in I} 2^{X^*_i}\setminus \emptyset}
\Big( \prod_{i \in I} \lambda_{x^*_i}(E_i) \Big)\rho(D \cup \bigcup_i E_i,E_j) & \text{if $x= x_j$ for some $j$}
\end{cases}.
\]
\end{comment}



\begin{lemma}\label{lem:ind}Given $\mu \in \Delta(\L(\tilde X \cup X^*))$ and an independent aggregation rule $\lambda$, we have for all $A \subseteq {\mathscr A}$ and $a \in A$
\[
\hat{\rho}(A,a)=  \sum_{(S_b)_{b \in {\mathscr A}}} 
\lambda ((S_b)_{b \in \A}) \cdot  \rho^\mu(\cup_{b \in A} S_b, S_a).
\]
where $\rho^\mu(\cup_{b \in A} S_b, S_a) = \mu(\succ \in \L (\tilde{X} \cup X^*) | \exists x \in S_a, \ \forall y \in \cup_{b \in A \setminus a} S_b, \ x \succ y)$
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} Fix $A \subseteq \A$ and $a \in A$. Then, $\hat{\rho}(A, a)
= \sum_{\left(S_b\right)_{b \in A} \in \prod_{b \in A} \left(2^{X(b)} \setminus \emptyset\right)} 
\lambda_A\left((S_b)_{b \in A}\right) \cdot 
\rho^\mu(\cup_{b \in A} S_b, S_a) 
= \sum_{\left(S_b\right)_{b \in A}} 
\rho^\mu(\cup_{b \in A} S_b, S_a) \cdot
\lambda \Big(\prod_{b \in A} (S_b) \times \prod_{b \in \A \setminus A} (2^{X(b)}\setminus \emptyset)\Big) 
= \Big(\sum_{\left(S_b\right)_{b \in B}} 
\rho(\cup_{b \in B} S_b, S_a)\Big) \cdot \Big(\sum_{(S_b)_{b \in \A \setminus B}}
\lambda\left((S_b)_{b \in \A}\right) \Big)
= \sum_{\left(S_b\right)_{b \in \A}} 
\lambda\left((S_b)_{b \in \A}\right) \cdot 
\rho(\cup_{b \in B} S_b, S_a)$.
\end{proof}



 Lemma \ref{lem:ind} means that aggregated stochastic choice $\hat{\rho}$ is a convex combination of $\rho^\mu$ for each composition of the aggregated alternatives. If $\rho^\mu$ is RUM, then $\hat{\rho}$ is also a RUM. This intuition is formalized in the following proposition.



\begin{proposition}  
A stochastic choice function $\hat{\rho}$ is RU rational with independent $\lambda$ if and only if $\hat{\rho}$ is aggregated RU rational.
\end{proposition}\label{thm:independent}

\begin{proof}
If direction: If  $\hat{\rho}$ is rationalizable by a random utility model $\hat{\mu}$ over $\A$, then as we showed in Proposition \ref{thm:non-overlapping} that there exists $(\mu,\lambda)$ that rationalizes $\hat \rho$ and $\mu$ is non-overlapping. Moreover, as proved in Lemma \ref{lem:non-overlapping} (a), for non-overlapping representation any $\lambda$ works; so we can choose an independent $\lambda$ as desired. 


Only if direction: Suppose that $\hat{\rho}$ is RU rationalizable with $(\mu, \lambda)$, where $\lambda$ is independent.  Let $\rho^\mu$ be the random utility function associated with $\mu$. Given $(S_a)_{a \in {\mathscr A}} \subseteq \prod _{a \in {\mathscr A}} X(a) $, for each $(B,b)$ such that $b \in B \subseteq {\mathscr A}$ define $\hat{\rho}_{(S_a)_{a \in {\mathscr A}}}(B,b)=\rho^\mu(\cup_{a \in B} S_a, S_b)$.\footnote{Note that $\hat{\rho}_{(S_a)_{a \in {\mathscr A}}}$ is a data set where $a$ always means $S_a$.} 
Given $(S_a)_{a \in {\mathscr A}} \subseteq \prod _{a \in {\mathscr A}} 2^{X(a)}\setminus \{\emptyset\} $, for each $\succ \in \mathcal{L} (\tilde{X} \cup X^*)$ define $\hat{\succ}_{(S_a)_{a \in \A}}$ by $a \succ b$ if and only if there exists $x \in S_a$ such that $x \succ y$ for all $y \in S_b$. Note that $\hat{\succ}_{(S_a)_{a \in \A}}$ is a linear order on ${\mathscr A}$. Now we define a probability measure $\hat{\mu}_{(S_a)_{a \in \A}}$ over linear orders on ${\mathscr A}$ as follows: for any linear order $\succ'$ on ${\mathscr A}$,  define 
 $\hat{\mu}_{(S_a)_{a \in \A}}(\succ') = \sum_{\succ \in {\mathscr L}(\tilde{X} \cup X^*): \hat{\succ}_{(S_a)_{a \in \A}} =\succ'}\mu(\succ)$.

Note that, by definition, $\hat{\mu}_{(S_a)_{a \in \A}}$ RU rationalizes $\hat{\rho}_{(S_a)_{a \in \A}}$, that is for any $(A,a)$ such that $a \in A \subseteq \A$, $
\hat{\rho}_{(S_a)_{a \in \A}}(A,a)=\hat{\mu}_{(S_a)_{a \in \A}}(\{\succ' \in {\mathscr L }({\mathscr A})|a \succ' b, \text{ for all } b \in A\setminus a\})$. Now let $\hat{\mu} = \sum_{(S_a)_{a \in {\mathscr A}}} \lambda((S_a)_{a \in \A}) \hat{\mu}_{(S_a)_{a \in \A}}$. Then  \begin{align*}
\hat{\rho}(B,a) &=  \sum_{(S_a)_{a \in {\mathscr A}}} \lambda((S_a)_{a \in \A})   \rho^\mu(\cup_{a \in B} S_a, S_a)  &(\because \text{Lemma }\ref{lem:ind})\\
&=\sum_{(S_a)_{a \in {\mathscr A}}} \lambda((S_a)_{a \in \A})  \hat{\mu}_{(S_a)_{a \in \A}}(\{\succ' \in {\mathscr L }({\mathscr A})|a \succ' b, \text{ for all } b \in A\setminus a\})\\
&=\hat{\mu}(\{\succ' \in {\mathscr L }({\mathscr A})|a \succ' b , \text{ for all } b \in A\setminus a\}).
\end{align*}
Thus $\hat{\mu}$ rationalizes $\hat{\rho}$.
