\section{Simulation}\label{sec:simulation}


The theoretical results from the previous section show that, in general, the implications of the random utility model (RUM) for the observed dataset~$\hat{\rho}$ are too weak to ensure that~$\hat{\rho}$ is aggregated RU rational—except under two special conditions: when the aggregation rule~$\lambda$ is menu independent, or when the underlying preference structure becomes non overlapping.


These results motivate the following conjecture:
\emph{As the aggregation rule~$\lambda$ deviates from menu independence or the underlying preferences deviate from the non-overlapping condition, the likelihood that~$\hat{\rho}$ can be rationalized by aggregated RUM diminishes. Consequently, estimation using standard RUM such as logit is expected to yield increasingly biased utility estimates.} This section empirically investigates this conjecture using simulation analysis.

Specifically, we generate true choice data~$\rho$ from a RUM, using a logit model with fixed utility values. Given~$\rho$ and a specified aggregation rule~$\lambda$, we construct the observed dataset~$\hat{\rho}$. We then estimate utility parameters from~$\hat{\rho}$ using the logit framework and assess the resulting estimation bias. In addition, we compute the distance from~$\hat{\rho}$ to the set of random utility models to evaluate the extent of rationality loss.

The theoretical predictions lead to two empirical hypotheses:
\begin{itemize}
\item As~$\lambda$ becomes less menu independent or preferences become more overlapping, the dataset~$\hat{\rho}$ is increasingly unlikely to be rationalized by aggregated RUM, let alone a logit model. This results in larger estimation biases. (See Section \ref{sec:bias} for details)
\item The distance from~$\hat{\rho}$ to the aggregated RUM polytope increases with greater deviation from menu independence or greater preference overlap. (See Section \ref{sec:distance} for details)
\end{itemize}
In the following, we verify these two hypothesis by simulations. 

\subsection{Setup}


Consider three alternatives $x,y,a_0$, where $x,y$ are non-aggregated alternatives; $a_0$ is an aggregated alternative (the outside option). The outside option can mean good $z$ or $w$. That is, $X(a_0)=\{z, w\}$.

We examine three distinct markets, each represented by one of the following choice sets: ${x, a_0}$, ${y, a_0}$, and ${x, y, a_0}$. Importantly, the composition of the aggregated alternative $a_0$ varies across these markets; that is, the aggregation functions $\lambda_{{x, a_0}}$, $\lambda_{{y, a_0}}$, and $\lambda_{{x, y, a_0}}$ are allowed to differ. Given fixed utilities values $u(x), u(y), u(z)$, and $u(w)$, we generate the true dataset $\rho$ by assuming the logit model: \[
\rho(D,x)= \frac{\exp (u(x))}{\sum_{j \in D}\exp (u(j))}.
\]

%\[
%\rho(D,x)= \dfrac{\exp (\theta_p p_x+ \theta_q q_x)}{\sum_{i \in D}\exp (\theta_p p_i+ \theta_q q_i)}.
%\]
Consider the aggregation rules $\lambda_{\{x, a_0\}}$, $\lambda_{\{y, a_0\}}$, and $\lambda_{\{x, y, a_0\}}$ over $\{\{w\}, \{z\}, \{w,z\}\}$. With $\rho$ and $\lambda$, we construct the observable reduced dataset $\hat{\rho}$ incorporating the aggregated alternatives.  That is  for any $D \subseteq \{x,y\}$ and $j \in D$, we define $\hat{\rho}(D \cup a_0, j)$ as 
\begin{align*}
\lambda_{D \cup a_0} (w) \rho(D\cup \{w\}, j) +\lambda_{D \cup a_0} (z) \rho(D\cup \{z\}, j)+\lambda_{D \cup a_0} (\{z,w\}) \rho(D\cup \{z,w\}, j).
\end{align*}
Given the observed aggregated dataset~$\hat{\rho}$, we estimate the utility parameters~$\hat{u}(x)$ and~$\hat{u}(y)$ under the assumption that~$\hat{\rho}$ follows a multinomial logit model and that the utility of the outside option is zero, i.e.,~$\exp(u(a_0)) = 1$.



Note that in this setup
\begin{itemize}
\item  The aggregation rule $\lambda$ becomes less {\it menu independent} as $\lambda_{\{x, a_0\}}$, $\lambda_{\{y, a_0\}}$, and $\lambda_{\{x, y, a_0\}}$ become more distinct.
\item Fixing the true utilities of $u(x)$ and $u(y)$—say, for simplicity, $u(x) > u(y)$—the preference structure becomes \emph{more overlapping} as $\max\{u(z), u(w)\}$ exceeds $u(x)$ and $\min\{u(z), u(w)\}$ falls below $u(y)$. 
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Bias}\label{sec:bias}

\subsubsection{Bias measure}
% As mentioned earlier, the theoretical results suggest that as the utility specification and the structure of $\lambda$ deviate from satisfying the conditions (A) and (B), the observable dataset $\hat{\rho}$ is increasingly unlikely to be rationalized by any random utility model---let alone by a logit model. Consequently, the estimated utilities are expected to become more biased. 
We quantify the estimation bias using the following measure:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bias}
\exp \left| (\hat{u}(x) - \hat{u}(y)) - (u(x) - u(y)) \right|,
\end{equation}
which captures the distortion in relative utilities between alternatives $x$ and $y$. To interpret this measure, consider the case in which $\hat{u}(x) - \hat{u}(y) > u(x) - u(y)$. Then the expression simplifies to
\[
\frac{\exp(\hat{u}(x))}{\exp(\hat{u}(y))} \Big/ \frac{\exp(u(x))}{\exp(u(y))},
\]
which represents the ratio of the estimated odds ratio to the true odds ratio of choosing $x$ over $y$. A deviation in this ratio reflects how the estimated preference ordering---and thus predicted behavior---diverges from the true underlying model. Therefore, our measure serves as a natural index of bias. 


\subsubsection{Effect of the aggregation function $\lambda$ on bias}

In the first simulation, we tested the following conjecture: {\it the bias defined in (\ref{eq:bias}) becomes larger as $\lambda$ become less menu independent (i.e., as $\lambda$ changes across choice sets $\{x,a_0\},\{y,a_0\}$, $\{x,y,a_0\}$).}  As the preference structure is not the focus of the analysis, we fixed the values $u(x)=-1$, $u(y)=-2$, $u(z)=1, u(w)=0$. (In the appendix, we tried other values of $u(x)$ and $u(y)$). 


