\subsection{Distance to the aggregated random utility model}\label{sec:distance}

\subsubsection{Distance measure}

To quantify the deviation of the reduced dataset $\hat{\rho}$ from the aggregated RUM polytope, we use the distance of $\hat{\rho}$ from the set of stochastic choice functions that are rationalizable by some aggregated random utility model. Formally, we consider the minimum distance---in a suitable norm---between $\hat{\rho}$ and the set $\mathcal{\hat{P}}_r$ of all stochastic choice rules that are consistent with an aggregated random utility representation.

We define the \emph{aggregated random utility polytope} as follows. Let $\mathcal{L}(\A)$ denote the set of all rankings  (i.e., linear orders) over the finite set of  alternatives $\A$. For each $\succ \in \mathcal{L}(\A)$, define the deterministic choice rule $\hat{\rho}^{\succ}$ by:
\[
\hat{\rho}^{\succ}(A, a) =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } a \succ b \text{ for all }b \in A \setminus a, \\
0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]
Then the random utility polytope is the convex hull of these deterministic rules:
\[
\mathcal{\hat{P}}_r = \left\{ \rho : \rho(A, a) = \sum_{\succ \in \mathcal{L}(\A)} \mu(\succ) \cdot \hat{\rho}^{\succ}(A, a) \quad \text{for all } A \subseteq \A,\ a \in A,\ \text{with } \mu
\in \Delta(\mathcal{L}(\A)) \right\},
\]
where $\Delta(\mathcal{L}(\A))$ denotes the probability simplex over $\mathcal{L}(\A)$. Thus, $\mathcal{\hat{P}}_r$ contains all stochastic choice rules that are rationalizable by an aggregate RUM. 

This definition captures the reduced-form implications of the random utility model without reference to cardinal utilities or specific functional forms like logit. A dataset $\hat{\rho}$ belongs to $\mathcal{\hat{P}}_r$ if and only if it is rationalizable by an aggregated random utility model. Hence, the distance from $\hat{\rho}$ to $\mathcal{\hat{P}}_r$ provides a natural metric for assessing the extent of violation of the aggregated random utility assumption.

%In our analysis, this distance complements utility estimation bias: while bias measures how far the inferred utilities are from the true ones, the distance to $\mathcal{P}_r$ captures how structurally inconsistent the observed dataset is with any aggregated random utility model. 

To see how the minimal distance can be defined in our setup, remember that we have three alternatives $x, y, a_0$. Thus the number of linear orders is $6$; the number of coordinate of $\hat{\rho}$ (i.e., $(D,x)$ such that $x \in D\subseteq \{x,y,a_0\}$) is 12. Then, the distance can be defined as follows: Given $\hat{\rho} \in \Re^{12}$, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:distance}
\min_{\mu \in \Delta^5} \left\| \hat{\rho} - \sum_{i=1}^6 \mu_i \hat{\rho}^{\succ_i} \right\|_2^2,
\end{equation}
where the norm is the Euclidian norm. Here, $\Delta^5$ is the five dimensional simplex and an element of the simplex satisfies $\mu \in \mathbb{R}^6$, $\mu_i \geq 0$, and $\sum_i \mu_i = 1$. 


% As we show theoretically, $\hat{\rho}$ may not follow an aggregated random utility model (let alone a logit model) as $\lambda$ becomes dependent, even though the true model $\rho$ is a logit model.  Empirically, we aim to show that our estimates $\hat{\theta}_p$ and $\hat{\theta}_q$ deviate more from the true parameters $\hat{\theta}_p$ and $\hat{\theta}_q$ as $\lambda$ becomes increasingly non-independent. Additionally, we are interested in quantifying the extent of the deviation between the estimates and the true parameters.  



