\section{Details of Simulations}
\subsection{Simulation 1}

In Simulation 1, we are changing $\lambda$ finding biases 
\begin{itemize}
\item We assume that true data $\rho$s are generated by logit models with $u(z)=0, u(w)=1$, and 3 versions of $(u(x), u(y))$: $(-1,-2)$, $(1,-1)$, and $(2,1)$.
\item We choose one of $\lambda_{x,y,a_0}, \lambda_{x,a_0}, \lambda_{y,a_0}$. Lets say $\lambda_{x,y,a_0}$. We fix the value of $\lambda_{x,y,a_0}(\{w\})$ and $\lambda_{x,y,a_0}(\{z\})$, which determines the value of $\lambda_{x,y,a_0}(z,w)$. Then we  find the value of biases for the three different case: 
\begin{itemize}
\item (1) independent case (i.e., $\lambda_{x,y,a_0}=\lambda_{x,a_0}=\lambda_{y,a_0}$) 
\item (2) minimal bias by changing all possible values of $\lambda_{x,a_0}$ and $\lambda_{y,a_0}$; 
\item (3) maximial bias by changing all possible values of $\lambda_{x,a_0}$ and $\lambda_{y,a_0}$  
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

We also created heatmaps in the following way: we construct a heatmap representation to visualize the sensitivity of inference bias to deviations from context-independence. Specifically, for each heatmap, we choose one of the attention weights---$\lambda_{x,a_0}$, $\lambda_{y,a_0}$, or $\lambda_{x,y,a_0}$---to be varied over a discrete grid of values satisfying $\lambda(\{z\}) + \lambda(\{w\}) + \lambda(z,w) = 1$, while holding the other two fixed at the following values:
\[
\lambda(\{z\}) = 0.3,\quad \lambda(\{w\}) = 0.2,\quad \lambda(z,w) = 0.5.
\]
Each grid point in the heatmap represents a specific assignment of $\lambda(\{z\})$ and $\lambda(\{w\})$, with $\lambda(z,w)$. The bias is computed at each grid point. The cell corresponding to the independent case---where all three attention weights are equal---is highlighted in blue. This allows us to visually benchmark the inference bias under independence against alternative dependent cases.


\subsection{Simulation 2}

In Simulation 2, we will change $\lambda$ to find distances.

\begin{itemize}
\item We assume that true data $\rho$s are generated by logit models with $u(z)=0, u(w)=1$, and 3 versions of $(u(x), u(y))$: $(-1,-2)$, $(1,-1)$, and $(2,1)$.
\item For each configuration and each test \(\lambda =(\lambda (\{z\}), \lambda (\{w\})) \in [0,1]^2 \) with \( \lambda (\{z\}) + \lambda (\{w\}) \leq 1\) we set \( \lambda (z,w) = 1 - \lambda (\{z\}) - \lambda (\{w\})\) . It is then applied to these frame-role assignments:
\begin{itemize}
\item original: only \( \lambda_{\{x,a_0\}} \) applies the test \( \lambda \).
\item Swap 12: only \( \lambda_{\{x,y,a_0\}} \) applies the test \( \lambda \)
\item Swap 23: only \(\lambda_{\{y,a_0\}}\) applies the test \( \lambda \).
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
All the other cases use baseline weights \( (0.1, 0.4, 0.5) \).

Let \( V = \{v^{1}, \dots, v^{(6)}\subseteq \mathbb{R}^{12} \) denote the RUM vertices associated with strict orders on \( \{x, y, z \} \). For each grid point, we compute the distance"
\[
\delta(\hat{\rho}) = \min_{\gamma \in \Delta^5} \left \|\hat{\rho} - \sum_{i =1}^6 \gamma_i v^{(i)} \right \|_2
\]
as the minimum Euclidean distance from \(\hat{\rho}\) to the RUM polytope.
We generate heatmaps over the test grid, with intensity representing \( \delta(\hat{\rho} \) and highlight the baseline configuration in cyan.

