\subsection{Monotonicity of $\lambda$}

Independence was too strong. An alternative condition is the following condition:


Weak  Monotonicity on $\lambda$:
For any $B,C \subset A$ and $a \in B,  C$ and $D, D'$ 
\[
\lambda_B(D(a)) \ge \lambda_B(D'(a)) \Leftrightarrow \lambda_C (D(a)) \ge \lambda_C (D'(a)). 
\]

The conditions mean that if $a$ means $D(a)$ more likely than $D'(a)$ in a choice set $B$; then  the same thing holds for another choice set $C$.

Note that this is weaker than the independence condition which requires that $\lambda_B(D(a))=\lambda_C(D(a))$.


Strong Monotonicity on $\lambda$:
For any $B,C \subset A$ and $a,b \in B,  C$ and $D, D'$ 
\[
\lambda_B(D(a)) \ge \lambda_B(D'(b)) \Leftrightarrow \lambda_C (D(a)) \ge \lambda_C (D'(b)). 
\]


Very Storong Monotonicity on $\lambda$:
For any $B,C \subset A$ such tha $B \subset C$ and $a,b \in B,  C$ and $D, D'$ 
\[
\lambda_B(\{D(a)\}_{a \in B}) \ge \lambda_B(\{D'(a)\}_{a \in B}) \Leftrightarrow \lambda_C (\{D(a)\}_{a \in B}) \ge \lambda_C (\{D'(b)\}_{a \in B}). 
\]


