The dynamics of filaments is simulated according to the following Langevin dynamics for each monomer:
\begin{equation}
m\ddot{\mathbf{r}}=\mathbf{f}_a-\gamma\dot{\mathbf{r}}-\nabla_{\mathbf{r}}U+\mathbf{R}(t)
\end{equation}
with $m$ as the monomer mass, $\mathbf{r}$ as the monomer position, $\mathbf{f}_a$ as the active force, $U$ as the potential energy whose gradient provides the conservative forces, $\gamma$ as the friction coefficient and $\mathbf{R}(t)$ as the zero mean Gaussian white thermal noise with the correlation function given by fluctuation-dissipation theorem $\langle\mathbf{R}_{i}(t)\cdot\mathbf{R}_{j}(t')\rangle=6\gamma k_{B}T\delta_{ij}\delta(t-t')$, where $k_{B}$ is the Boltzmann constant , $T$ is the temperature, $i$ and $j$ indicates any two of the monomers. The interaction potential energy includes three parts as the bond potential between each neighboring monomers for the same filament, angle potential between each neighboring bonds and non-bonded volume-excluded potential between all pairs of monomers of filaments. The bond stretching is controlled by a FENE potential\cite{fene_1990} $U_{bond}(r)=-k_{b}R_{0}^{2}\ln\left[1-(r-\Delta)^2/R_0^2\right]/2$ with bond strength $k_{b}$, equilibrium bond length $\Delta$ and maximum fluctuation $R_{0}$. The angle potential is given by harmonic form $U_{angle}(\vartheta)=\kappa(\pi-\vartheta)^{2}$ with filament bending modulus $\kappa$. Here $\vartheta$ is the angle between each neighboring bonds.  The volume excluded potential avoids overlap of non-bonded monomers and effectively serve as the local nematic alignment. Its form is taken from Weeks-Chandler-Anderson potential as the truncated repulsive part of the Lennard Jones potential~\cite{Weeks1971}: $U_{nb}(r)=4\epsilon\left[\left(\sigma/r\right)^{12}-\left(\sigma/r\right)^{6}+1/4\right]\vartheta(2^{1/6}\sigma-r)$. where $\epsilon$ controls the strength of steric repulsion and $\vartheta(x)$ is the Heaviside function specifying the cutoff length. Finally, the activity is modeled by self-propulsion force on each monomer directed along the filament tangent toward its head:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{f}_a=\eta_\alpha(t) f_{a}\, b \,\text{\textbf{t}}
\end{equation}
where $b$ is the bond length and $\text{\textbf{t}}$ is the unit tangent vector of each monomer, representing the direction of the active force. This active force is shared equally between $i$th and $(i+1)$th monomer as $f_{a}/2$, where $f_{a}$ stands for the strength of activity. In order to simulate active nematics, the active force is also nematics as it reverses the direction of active force at stochastic time intervals, which effectively switches the head and tail of the filaments. This is reached by $\eta_\alpha(t)$, which changes its value between 1 and -1 at Poisson distributed time intervals with averaged value $\tau$, so that $\tau$ is the time scale controlling the reversal frequency. The illustration of one individual active filament and the schematic representation of the bead-spring model is represented in Fig.~\ref{fig:pheno}. Integration of Langevin dynamics was facilitated by a modified version of LAMMPS \cite{LAMMPS}, which incorporated the necessary functions for active force. \\
\indent Within the LAMMPS framework, the LJ unit system was employed, wherein the length unit corresponded to the diameter of a monomer, the energy unit corresponded to $k_B T$, and the mass unit corresponded to the mass of a monomer. We explored a wide range of stiffness (from 100 to 1500) and activity (from 1 to 10),  effectively encompassing from chaotic to nearly static flow states. To explore the bulk properties, a periodic boundary condition was implemented, while an initial condition of perfect ordered state was chosen to delve into the instability leading to active turbulence. The relative displacement between each pair of bonded monomers is considered as a nematic micro-agents $\norm{u}$. Coarse-graining $\norm{u}$ provides the tensor order parameter field $\tens{Q}$, whose largest eigenvalue and the corresponding eigenvector is $S$ and $\nd$, respectively. Elaboration into the governing equations with parameters and the method of coarse-graining can be found in the supplementary information (SI). The simple illustration of the active semi-flexible filament is demonstrated in Fig.1.

\indent Beyond its geometric structure, the quasi-2D configuration also manifests in system dynamics. The elastic deformation of the $\nd$ field is characterized by the dominant wave vector $\norm{k}$ during instability~\cite{kleman_soft_2003, Elias_2016}. In our simulations, bend instability corresponds to $\norm{k}$ aligning with the unperturbed order $\nd_0$, indicating that fundamental dynamics also occur on principal planes, where $\nd_0$, $\delta\nd$, $\norm{k}$, and the unit active force all lie. \\

