The Gaussian centroid of the PSF moves throughout the visit due to movement of the JWST pointing. This can manifest in two components, each representing movement along a perpendicular axes (Figure \ref{fig:HD260655b centroiding}).

In the majority of visits we investigated, the positive-diagonal axis is the 2nd highest ranked component. For brighter stars the positive-diagonal centroiding component represents the most variance. The 2nd perpendicular centroiding component (negative-diagonal) is always ranked below the positive-diagonal component; in the dimmest star analyzed (TRAPPIST-1) it does not appear in the top 5 highest ranked components, while in the brightest star analyzed (HD260655b) it appears as the 2nd strongest component in all visits. The rank of these components increases as the brightness of the star increases.

